INT. UNDERTAKER'S PARLOUR - BACK ROOM - NIGHT - 1927

Behind a velvet curtain, a secret card game. Six coffins stand in a row against the wall, lids propped open as stools. A card table in the middle. Smoke hangs under a green-shaded lamp. A gramophone plays a tinny foxtrot.

MAUDE PELLETIER (50s, owner, black bombazine, rings on every finger) deals cards with a flick of her wrist.

CYRIL OSGOOD (30s, bookkeeper, spectacles, sweat on his lip) arranges his hand in a fan.

OTTOLINE VANTERPOOL (20s, flapper, fur collar, cigarette holder) leans back in a coffin as if it were a chaise longue.

BRAM HOLLIDAY (40s, bootlegger, pinstripes, a pistol bulge under his arm) drinks gin out of a tin funeral urn.

The curtain parts. A man in a rain-soaked fedora steps through. A STRANGER (30s, slim, quiet, hands in his pockets).

MAUDE
We're closed, mister.

STRANGER
The door said open.

MAUDE
The door says a lot of things.

STRANGER
I heard there was a game.

BRAM
You heard wrong.

OTTOLINE
Oh, let him sit, Bram. He's pretty.

CYRIL
(swallowing)
Is he police?

STRANGER
No.

BRAM
You'd say that.

STRANGER
I would.

He takes a seat in the empty coffin, folding his coat over his knees. Maude deals him in without waiting for permission. He turns up his cards, studies them, and sets them face-down.

MAUDE
What's your name?

STRANGER
Does it matter?

MAUDE
Everyone who sits here has one.

Ottoline blows smoke at the lamp. Cyril clears his throat, drops a chip. Bram watches the Stranger over the rim of the urn.

STRANGER
Nolan.

MAUDE
Just Nolan?

NOLAN
It's what the boys at the dock call me.

Bram's urn stops halfway to his mouth.

BRAM
Which dock?

NOLAN
Pier nine.

BRAM
Pier nine's mine.

NOLAN
I know.

The gramophone needle hits the end of the record and ticks. Nobody gets up to change it. Cyril's hand goes to the little pearl-handled derringer in his boot. He thinks better of it.

OTTOLINE
Oh, I do love a scene.

BRAM
Shut it, Otty.

OTTOLINE
You shut it. I'm having a lovely time.

NOLAN
(to the table)
Nobody has to get hurt.

MAUDE
Good. I've hurt enough people this week.

BRAM
You think you can walk in here and--

CYRIL
I think we should all calm down--

OTTOLINE
I think he should deal--

MAUDE
Quiet!

They stop. Maude lays down the deck. Slides one coffin lid shut with a bang that makes the lamp swing.

MAUDE (CONT'D)
(to Nolan)
Say your piece.

NOLAN
Mr. Holliday's been shorting Pier nine for six months. The Chief says the count's off by forty crates.

BRAM
Who's the Chief?

NOLAN
The man whose pier it really is.

BRAM
That's a lie.

^NOLAN
That's a receipt.

He lays a folded paper on the felt. Maude picks it up, scans it, passes it to Cyril. Cyril reads and goes grey.

CYRIL
(whispering)
Bram. It's your signature.

BRAM
It's forged.

CYRIL
It's got the thumb.

BRAM
Give me that!

He lunges. Chips fly. The urn clatters off. Ottoline shrieks with laughter and scrambles backward. Nolan doesn't move from his coffin. Bram's pistol is in his hand now, shaking, aimed at Nolan's chest.

MAUDE
Bram, put it down.

BRAM
He's not leaving.

NOLAN
Not through the door, no.

Bram blinks. The curtain behind him moves. A shadow stretches along the wall.

Maude stands. Her chair scrapes.

MAUDE
Bram. Behind you.

He half turns. Maude's ringed hand takes the pistol out of his grip, neat as a waiter lifting a plate. She holds it by the barrel.

The gramophone needle skips. Silence.

OTTOLINE
(delighted)
Oh, Maude's the Chief!

Nolan collects his cards. Turns them over. A full house.

NOLAN
Maude. Your deal.

MAUDE
(dryly)
Of course it is.

FADE OUT.
